# islam.museum games: an offline copy for independent testing

This folder is the three games exactly as they are on the museum's test host, with everything they fetch, so that they can be played with no internet at all.

| | |
|---|---|
| Release | `@RELEASE@` |
| Source commit | `@COMMIT@` (committed @COMMIT_DATE@) |
| Taken from | the build artifact of that commit (GitHub Actions run @BUILD_RUN@); `site/release.json` is byte for byte the file the test host serves |
| Made | @MADE@ |
| Size | @FILES@ files, @BYTES@ bytes |

Nothing in the games was changed for this package. No credential, key or token is in it.

## Start

Python 3 is all it needs. Nothing is installed and nothing is fetched.

```
python3 serve.py
```

Then open, in a browser on the same computer:

| Game | Address |
|---|---|
| Granada | http://localhost:8765/granada/?lang=en&fresh=1 |
| The Golden Age, chapter 1 | http://localhost:8765/golden-age/?lang=en&fresh=1 |
| The Golden Age, chapter 2 | http://localhost:8765/golden-age/?lang=en&scenario=house-of-books&fresh=1 |
| The Golden Age, chapter 3 | http://localhost:8765/golden-age/?lang=en&scenario=what-endures&fresh=1 |
| Atlas | http://localhost:8765/atlas/?lang=en |

`python3 serve.py 9000` uses another port. Use `localhost` (or `127.0.0.1`), not the machine's network address: browsers offer WebGPU only to `https` and to `localhost`.

The server listens on this computer only, answers GET and HEAD, reads nothing outside `site/`, and sends the same security headers as the test host (recorded in `host-headers.json`), so the pages run under the same content security policy. `http://localhost:8765/_channel` answers what the test host's `/_channel` answered when the package was made.

## Languages

Change `lang=en` to `ar`, `ur`, `fr`, `es`, `tr` or `id`. Arabic and Urdu read right to left; the land and the globe are never mirrored.

## 3D and the fallbacks

The 3D views need WebGPU. Without it each game plays in its fallback, and that is by design, not a fault.

| To get | Add to the address | Games |
|---|---|---|
| The fallback only (drawn plan, plan view, drawn globe) | `&no3d=1` | all three |
| Granada starting on its plan, with 3D one button away | `&view=plan` | Granada |
| A lighter 3D picture (no post-processing) | `&q=low` | Granada, The Golden Age |
| Granada without its fetched models (its own simpler shapes) | `&models=off` | Granada |
| No music | `&music=off` | all three |

How to tell which you are seeing:

- Granada: the buttons "3D view" and "Plan" appear above the picture when 3D is on. In the console, `__GR__.threeD` is `on`, `off` or `failed: …`.
- The Golden Age: in the console, `__GA__.no3d` is absent in 3D and a reason otherwise.
- Atlas: `document.documentElement.dataset.globe` is `3d` or `2d`.

**To force 3D in a browser that has WebGPU switched off** (many headless and virtual machines do), these Chromium flags may help; they were not tested in your environment and cannot make a GPU that is not there:
`--enable-unsafe-webgpu --enable-features=Vulkan --use-angle=swiftshader --use-webgpu-adapter=swiftshader --ignore-gpu-blocklist`.
If WebGPU cannot be had, review the fallbacks and say that the 3D was not seen.

## The same game every time

| Add | Effect |
|---|---|
| `&fresh=1` | starts a new game instead of resuming the saved one |
| `&seed=7` | Granada: the same weather every time. The Golden Age: the same district |
| `&date=2026-12-01` | Atlas: the daily game of that date, the same five rounds for everyone |

Saves are in the browser's own storage for `localhost:8765`. A private window, or `fresh=1`, starts clean.

## What should be there

`EXPECTED.md` lists each recent change as a claim to check, with how to see it.

## The checks

`tests/qa-check.mjs` opens every game in a fresh browser context and writes down what happens. It needs Node and a Playwright that is already installed; it installs nothing.

```
python3 serve.py &
node tests/qa-check.mjs http://localhost:8765 en captures
```

It fails if any game does not start, if a forced mode does not do what it says, if a saved game does not resume, or **if any request is made to an address other than this computer's**. It is a functional check and not a review of the games.

`tests/reference/` holds the museum's own play scripts as they are in its repository. They are there to be read: they need that repository's workspace to run, and are not runnable from this folder.

`VERIFIED.json` is what `qa-check.mjs` found when this package was made, on the maker's machine, with every request to another address refused.

## Checksums

`SHA256SUMS` lists every file here. `site/release.json` is the release's own manifest: every built file with its size and SHA-256.

```
shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS      # macOS
sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS          # Linux
```

## What is in it

```
serve.py              the launcher
host-headers.json     the test host's security headers and its /_channel, as recorded
site/                 the release: granada/, golden-age/, atlas/, assets/, release.json
site/media/music/     the six tracks the games play (the museum's own suite)
site/media/models/    the model the valley fetches (the watermill), with its textures
EXPECTED.md           what each recent change should look like
tests/                the check, and the museum's play scripts for reference
VERIFIED.json         the check's result when the package was made
MANIFEST.json         where each part came from, with checksums of the sources' manifests
SHA256SUMS            every file
```

The games use the device's own fonts; no font file is fetched. Their shaders are generated by the game's own script at run time; there are no separate shader files. The eight other games of the platform have no code and are not here.
